17592186432452 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432452 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432452 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432452 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d9f495e7-479d-548e-a8f4-c607f00c6814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432453 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432453 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154716" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432453 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432453 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0c/0c5a57e090562c65ff7f0ff8d7c63f469a038d4396bba68be3cb4916f4e7aab7.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $\\overset{^}{COE}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432453 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04351232-e12f-56c5-9996-c18094808c54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432453 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/18/18be9d5946a70aa62b4dda5408fa7e3312fe2e33675cbf695cbbdecac2feaa5b.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\overset{^}{COD}$ and $\\overset{^}{DOE}$ are adjacent because they share the same side $\\overset{\\to }{OD}.$\n\nWe can add the measures of two adjacent angles to find the measure of the combined angle.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\overset{^}{COE} & =m\\overset{^}{COD}+m\\overset{^}{DOE} \\\\  & ={70}^{∘}+{35}^{∘} \\\\  & ={105}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432454 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432454 #edb/ref 1069 "q-150765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432454 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432454 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d8/d8552636c6f68b4885bf8a30bb8611629c0891ddc8c61cd27ff43c95cf6057bd.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $m∠BAD={70}^{∘}.$ What equation could be used to find the value of $x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432454 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22b396b3-caeb-5440-bb61-ec5206652aa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432454 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $∠BAC.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The angles $∠BAC$ and $∠CAD$ are adjacent because they share the side $\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$\n\n- So, the measure of the *large* angle $(∠BAD)$ must be the *sum* of the measures of the two *smaller* angles $(∠BAC$ and $∠CAD).$\n\n- This means that the measure of $∠BAC$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠BAD)$ and the smaller known angle $(∠CAD).$\n\nTherefore, the correct equation to determine $x$ is the following:\n\n$$\nx={70}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432455 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432455 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432455 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432455 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7e/7e365bb8c51729748b0efdb54f50459859a382cba1def4b32b4009b3bde89dbd.png)\n\nIn the figure above, find $m∠COE.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432455 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39939039-dfca-5f99-86f6-773331c4950d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432455 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/ae/ae007a68607a96bfd78986eeae11e69a4c8bd8dba35e8997933885d2ff1dc29f.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $∠COD$ and $∠DOE$ are adjacent because they share the side $\\overset{\\to }{OD}.$\n\nWe can add the measures of two adjacent angles to find the measure of the combined angle.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COE & =m∠COD+m∠DOE \\\\  & ={45}^{∘}+{59}^{∘} \\\\  & ={104}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432456 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432456 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432456 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432456 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e3/e3fe548e2dd695d682dec13287307e58fe28aa8a01b3e672695ef8ed07c61e1d.png)\n\nThe measure of the angle $∠BAE$ is ${150}^{∘}.$ Find the value of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432456 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "461025dd-2d02-5f63-a292-225c3460091c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432456 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $∠DAE.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The angles $∠BAD$ and $∠DAE$ are adjacent because they share the side $\\overset{\\to }{AD}.$\n\n- So, the measure of the *large* angle $(∠BAE)$ must be the *sum* of the measures of the two *smaller* angles $(∠BAD$ and $∠DAE).$\n\n- This means that the measure of $∠DAE$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠BAE)$ and the smaller known angle $(∠BAD).$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nx={150}^{∘}-{110}^{∘}={40}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432457 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432457 #edb/ref 1069 "q-150760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432457 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432457 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/17/17f25c6b6aa718b37a539f030a51b71ae12b7fa95aeb00fb68fa108e3a51df64.png)\n\nIn the figure above, find $m∠ROT.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432457 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5dfae6a6-b61d-556e-b52c-ca5836e31d8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432457 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/02/0269db77d9a551b6f75e260bac9d3385d6cfad05d15359bff6f059e0050ef1dc.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $∠ROS$ and $∠SOT$ are adjacent because they share the same side $\\overset{\\to }{OS}.$\n\nWe can add the measures of two adjacent angles to find the measure of the combined angle.\n\nTherefore: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠ROT & =m∠ROS+m∠SOT \\\\  & ={55}^{∘}+{66}^{∘} \\\\  & ={121}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432458 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432458 #edb/ref 1069 "q-4320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432458 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432458 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1e/1ed3b825702e9d446754d5bf56ff2964f8456bce45ab57fe9f6931458c9f5038.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $m∠DEA={147}^{∘}.$ Find the value of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432458 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a3a7198-9475-534b-8bac-8c66e26d7479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432458 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $∠CED.$\n\nThe measure of $∠CED$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠DEA)$ and the two smaller known angles $(∠CEB$ and $∠BEA).$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nx={147}^{∘}-{56}^{∘}-{40}^{∘}={51}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432459 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432459 #edb/ref 1069 "q-150767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432459 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432459 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/de/de5601836d150d370b9ab7b8d0dceb76bcd0174f77f930f01fb488b772fcbcc6.png)\n\nThe measure of the angle $∠BAD$ is ${100}^{∘}.$ Find the value of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432459 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c5fb27fa-76aa-58be-a097-af719acb9917" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432459 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $∠CAD.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The angles $∠BAC$ and $∠CAD$ are adjacent because they share the side $\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$\n\n- So, the measure of the *large* angle $(∠BAD)$ must be the *sum* of the measures of the two *smaller* angles $(∠BAC$ and $∠CAD).$\n\n- This means that the measure of $∠CAD$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠BAD)$ and the smaller known angle $(∠BAC).$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx={100}^{∘}-{70}^{∘}={30}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432460 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432460 #edb/ref 1069 "q-4319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432460 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432460 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/16/1650402ae8691f2578dc491516b074c20759afc0a6b848e003c03bad104fa8a5.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $m∠AFE={155}^{∘}.$ Find the value of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432460 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d11aa747-bb6b-58c1-bf80-7ecc88be04aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432460 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $∠DFE.$\n\nThe measure of $∠DFE$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠AFE)$ and the two smaller known angles $(∠AFC$ and $∠CFD).$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nx={155}^{∘}-{46}^{∘}-{65}^{∘}={44}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432461 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432461 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432461 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cd/cdd582f661cc05dd7e11cd6e104ccccb75e0b008b1144c1d1e6a97c7b07b7a1e.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of  $\\overset{^}{BOD}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432461 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8c7b1f1-384a-5a9f-b6ee-9ff5d00c5514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432461 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/e1/e1b8785260860f4946a7d0ca93a78fa2bddb5cbea95bcd841c75b3f53b5f1890.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\overset{^}{BOC}$ and $\\overset{^}{COD}$ are adjacent because they share the same side $\\overset{\\to }{OC}.$\n\nWe can add the measures of two adjacent angles to find the measure of the combined angle.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\overset{^}{BOD} & =m\\overset{^}{BOC}+m\\overset{^}{COD} \\\\  & ={100}^{∘}+{70}^{∘} \\\\  & ={170}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432462 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432462 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432462 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432462 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/89/89a4eb747c82276f349350f48ac2b711e1a308702eb1c717fbeacf79dbdad84f.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $m∠BAE={185}^{∘},$ $m∠BAD={120}^{∘},$ $m∠CAD={70}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$m∠CAE=$\n{{field-1}} ${}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432462